#
#     developer ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL
#
# Not inferred from a listing. Verified executable on 2026-09-19:
#
#     $ ssh aizu 'sudo -n /usr/bin/id -u'
#     0
#
# That is strictly worse than the grant it superseded: the old one was passwordless root
# through one named script, this is passwordless root through anything. Every scoped grant on
# this host, including the `PASSWD:` line above, is cosmetic while it exists — the only reason
# that line still prompts is load order, which is the note beside it.
#
# THE TARGET OF THE OLD GRANT IS STILL HAZARDOUS IN SHAPE. /opt/hbd/deploy-payme.sh remains
# `developer:developer 0755` inside a `developer`-owned /opt/hbd, so re-adding a grant for it
# would reopen the same hole. The rule that closed it stands, and it is the rule, not the
# instance, that matters: THE TARGET MUST NOT BE WRITABLE BY THE ACCOUNT BEING GRANTED. Note
# that /opt/bayram and /opt/bayram/sbin/bayram-release are correctly root:root, which is why
# this file installs the release script there and not into the old /opt/hbd tree.
#
# REMOVING THE BLANKET RULE WILL NOT LOCK ANYONE OUT — checked before recommending it.
# `passwd -S developer` reports `developer P ...` and /etc/shadow holds a real yescrypt hash,
# so the `(ALL:ALL) ALL` password path works. This matters because it is the ONLY root path
# left: /root/.ssh/authorized_keys is zero bytes and sshd reports `permitrootlogin
# without-password`.
#
# It IS a behaviour change for humans, not only hygiene: every ad-hoc `ssh aizu 'sudo ...'`
# without `-t` starts failing on a password prompt it cannot answer. That is why
# deploy/prune-stale-sudo-grants.sh puts the removal behind its own `--drop-blanket` flag —
# the clutter cleanup is not held hostage to that decision, and the two acts are separately
# reversible.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO GRANTS THAT NEEDED A DECISION — both answered 2026-09-19
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   (root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/tee /etc/bayram/payme.env      -> REMOVED
#     Four checks say it is NOT an escalation: /etc/bayram is root:root 0755 so the path
#     cannot be swapped, the argument is exact so the target cannot be redirected,
#     /usr/bin/tee is root:root, and the consumer bayram-payme.service runs `User=hbd
#     NoNewPrivileges=true` reading the path from `Environment=BAYRAM_PAYME_ENV_FILE=` rather
#     than systemd `EnvironmentFile=`. It was removed anyway, as an INTEGRITY grant over the
#     payment boundary: it is a standing, passwordless ability to replace the Payme merchant
#     verification key, and whoever sets that key can forge inbound callbacks against our own
#     ledger. Second, smaller reason: if the file were ever absent, `tee` would create it at
#     0666 & umask and publish the cashbox key world-readable. Full note, with the line to put
#     it back, in deploy/sudoers.d/bayram-deploy.
#
#   (postgres) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pg_dump hbd                -> NOTHING DEPENDS ON IT
#     The "what is deliberately NOT granted" section above argues against exactly this grant,
#     and it is live in /etc/sudoers.d/hbd-deploy. The 2026-09-19 survey went looking for the
#     caller that needs it and there is none: deploy/bayram-release requires EUID 0 before it
#     reaches its own `sudo -u postgres pg_dump`, deploy/install-bayram-backup.sh likewise
#     requires root before both its probe and its dump, and deploy-support.sh, deploy-d17.sh,
#     deploy-payme.sh and cutover-to-bayram.sh are all `Run as root, once: sudo bash ...` —
#     the first three now carrying `RETIRED 2026-09-19 — superseded by deploy/bayram-release.
#     DO NOT RUN.` A process already at uid 0 needs no sudoers entry to become postgres. The
#     nightly backup timer is not a consumer either: it is not installed on this host at all
#     (/etc/systemd/system/bayram-backup* does not exist; the only backup timer running is
#     aizu-backup.timer, a different product).
#     So this is not "consider removing" any more — nothing breaks when it goes. It is still
#     an OWNER DECISION rather than a cleanup, because docs/deployment/08-payme.md documents
#     it in several places as the sanctioned read path into production data for debugging.
#     Removing it is a workflow change as well as a security fix, which is why
#     deploy/prune-stale-sudo-grants.sh keeps it by default and deletes it only on
#     `--drop-pg-dump`.
#
# None of the above should be changed while you are the only session on the box. Keep a second
# root shell open, as the validation section says.

# ============================================================================
# THE LAYOUT THIS GRANT ASSUMES — nothing in the repo creates it. Run once, as root.
# ============================================================================
#   install -d -m 0755 -o root -g root      /opt/bayram/sbin
#   install -d -m 0755 -o root -g root      /opt/bayram/wheels
#   install -d -m 0700 -o root -g root      /opt/bayram/release/work
#   install -d -m 0700 -o root -g root      /var/backups/bayram
#
#   # 0770 root:developer — the operator must be able to WRITE a bundle here, and this is the
#   # only place they can. It is also, in the absence of signing, the whole of the authenticity
#   # boundary: anyone who can write here can hand the release script a bundle it will trust.
#   install -d -m 0770 -o root -g developer /opt/bayram/release/incoming
#
#   install -m 0755 -o root -g root deploy/bayram-release /opt/bayram/sbin/bayram-release
#
# Note the script lives in /opt/bayram/sbin and NOT in /usr/local/sbin. systemd's default PATH
# begins /usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin, and on Debian /usr/local is group-writable by `staff`
# on some installs — a root-invoked script sitting there is a privilege hazard for anyone in
# that group.
